{
  "concepts": [
    {
      "conditions": [],
      "definition": "Review at the sprint-close boundary under the authority cited by the manifest. It classifies reviewed items and debt entries, checks durable evidence and changed surfaces, and determines required durable follow-up.",
      "exceptions": [],
      "grounding": [
        {
          "end_line":277,
          "quote":"Sprint close begins by moving the manifest to `status: closing`. While\nclosing: new iteration inside that sprint is frozen; every included ticket\nor iteration entry is reviewed against the manifest; accumulated evidence\nis checked for durability; changed surfaces are walked against cited\nauthority; deferred compliance is classified; paydown tickets are opened\nwhere debt must survive past the close review (P6, P4b; rc.2\n#sprint-close-review).",
          "section_id":"repo://stdo-v3-generation/claude/9aee01ffa76cfbde1a6b8f1153a25bd6d71c216e/source/standards/TICKETS.md#TICKETS%2F3.%20Sprint%20rules%2FClose%20review",
          "start_line":271
        }
      ],
      "key":"sprint_close_review",
      "label":"Sprint close review"
    },
    {
      "conditions": [],
      "definition": "Item outcomes are accepted, local_paydown, design_reframe, requirement_reprice, product_reprice and remove. For the predicate that the item is accepted as compliant under sprint authority, accepted maps to accept/satisfied; local_paydown to local_repair/falsified; the three repricing outcomes to re_enter/invalid_basis; remove to falsified with no matching disposition. Debt-entry outcomes accepted, ticketed, repriced and removed have the corresponding mappings. A repricing layer is a declared change-class fact accompanying the core value as domain outcome.",
      "exceptions": [],
      "grounding": [
        {
          "end_line":312,
          "quote":"| `remove` | none | falsified | the work is backed out or kept out of closure; the reason for removal is the counterexample. No disposition of ACTIVATION §3 has this condition, because removal does not require a violated governing relation or a competing truth path |",
          "section_id":"repo://stdo-v3-generation/claude/9aee01ffa76cfbde1a6b8f1153a25bd6d71c216e/source/standards/TICKETS.md#TICKETS%2F3.%20Sprint%20rules%2FClose%20review",
          "start_line":312
        },
        {
          "end_line":329,
          "quote":"The mappings\nadd no disposition and no core value. The condition for `closed`\nabove requires an outcome for every item and debt entry, not a\n`satisfied` one. The mappings are new in this corpus.",
          "section_id":"repo://stdo-v3-generation/claude/9aee01ffa76cfbde1a6b8f1153a25bd6d71c216e/source/standards/TICKETS.md#TICKETS%2F3.%20Sprint%20rules%2FClose%20review",
          "start_line":326
        }
      ],
      "key":"close_outcome_domain",
      "label":"Close classifications and debt outcomes"
    },
    {
      "conditions": [],
      "definition": "A durable unit of work with one authoritative record. Its default carrier is one Markdown file; contract, history, state transitions, stable identity, searchability and closure semantics belong to the record irrespective of carrier.",
      "exceptions": [],
      "grounding": [
        {
          "end_line":342,
          "quote":"Each ticket has one durable authoritative record; the default carrier is\none markdown file. A repository or external tracker carrier is lawful only\nwhen it preserves the required ticket contract, history, state transition,\nstable identity, searchability and closure semantics without a co-equal\nlocal copy",
          "section_id":"repo://stdo-v3-generation/claude/9aee01ffa76cfbde1a6b8f1153a25bd6d71c216e/source/standards/TICKETS.md#TICKETS%2F4.%20Ticket%20rules%2FOne%20durable%20record%20per%20ticket",
          "start_line":338
        }
      ],
      "key":"ticket_record",
      "label":"Ticket authoritative record"
    },
    {
      "conditions": [],
      "definition": "One admitted upstream work item with more than one build-tenant execution line. Upstream authority and tenant-local tickets have distinct identities and lifecycles. This member owns the work-item/ticket-lifecycle relation in urn:stdo:bounded-context:build-tenancy; PRODUCT_DEFINITION §8 owns tenant identity and realisation meaning.",
      "exceptions": [],
      "grounding": [
        {
          "end_line":359,
          "quote":"A multi-build-tenant work item is one admitted upstream work item with more\nthan one build-tenant execution line. The upstream authority and each\ntenant-local ticket retain distinct identities and lifecycles.",
          "section_id":"repo://stdo-v3-generation/claude/9aee01ffa76cfbde1a6b8f1153a25bd6d71c216e/source/standards/TICKETS.md#TICKETS%2F4.%20Ticket%20rules%2FMulti-build-tenant%20independence",
          "start_line":357
        }
      ],
      "key":"multi_tenant_work_item",
      "label":"Multi-build-tenant work item"
    },
    {
      "conditions": [],
      "definition": "Type identifies the kind of work record. The minimum common population is feature, bug, spike and chore; projects may add types, and the default set is expected to suffice for most cases.",
      "exceptions": [],
      "grounding": [
        {
          "end_line":423,
          "quote":"The minimum common set of ticket types is `feature`, `bug`, `spike` and\n`chore`. A project may add more; the default set should be enough for\nmost cases\n(P3; rc.2 #allowed-types).",
          "section_id":"repo://stdo-v3-generation/claude/9aee01ffa76cfbde1a6b8f1153a25bd6d71c216e/source/standards/TICKETS.md#TICKETS%2F4.%20Ticket%20rules%2FTypes",
          "start_line":420
        }
      ],
      "key":"ticket_type",
      "label":"Ticket type"
    },
    {
      "conditions": [],
      "definition": "Category specifies execution or migration discipline. Its minimum common population is ordinary and implementation_migration. Implementation migration is governed replacement of an implementation truth path across producers, consumers, projections and proof surfaces; examples include replacing a tracker/read-model family, migrating every producer and consumer to a public interface before closure, and removing an authoritative bridge or compatibility facade.",
      "exceptions": [],
      "grounding": [
        {
          "end_line":438,
          "quote":"`implementation_migration` is\nused when the ticket is not \"change code until tests pass\" but a governed\nreplacement of one implementation truth path with another across producers,\nconsumers, projections and proof surfaces:",
          "section_id":"repo://stdo-v3-generation/claude/9aee01ffa76cfbde1a6b8f1153a25bd6d71c216e/source/standards/TICKETS.md#TICKETS%2F4.%20Ticket%20rules%2FCategory",
          "start_line":431
        }
      ],
      "key":"ticket_category",
      "label":"Ticket category"
    },
    {
      "conditions": [],
      "definition": "The intake-triage declaration of where a change lawfully re-enters the constitutional chain, using SPEC_METHOD §4 classes. Common, nonexclusive pairings are feature with goal_reprice, intent_reprice, product_reprice, requirement_reprice or design_reframe; bug with requirement_reprice, design_reframe or realization_refactor; chore with design_reframe or realization_refactor. A spike remains exploratory while carrying its current triage class.",
      "exceptions": [],
      "grounding": [
        {
          "end_line":464,
          "quote":"Ticket type and change class are orthogonal: type answers what kind of\nwork record this is; change class answers where the change lawfully\nre-enters the constitutional chain. A ticket records the `change_class`\ndeclared by intake triage, using the classes of\n[SPEC_METHOD §4](SPEC_METHOD.md#change-classes) (P4b, P2; rc.2\n#change-class).",
          "section_id":"repo://stdo-v3-generation/claude/9aee01ffa76cfbde1a6b8f1153a25bd6d71c216e/source/standards/TICKETS.md#TICKETS%2F4.%20Ticket%20rules%2FChange%20class",
          "start_line":459
        }
      ],
      "key":"ticket_change_class",
      "label":"Ticket change class"
    },
    {
      "conditions": [],
      "definition": "A source-described triage operation consuming computed classification for owner/layer traversal, complete relevant public membership and value delta, accepted-design scope, trace coverage and rule-determined treatment. For bugs it walks upward from the realised surface and associates the highest missing or insufficient governing layer with its lawful re-entry class.",
      "exceptions": [],
      "grounding": [
        {
          "end_line":488,
          "quote":"Triage consumes the computed classification of\n[SPEC_METHOD §4](SPEC_METHOD.md#computed-classification-and-treatment) for\nthe declared owner and layer traversal, the complete relevant public\nmembership and value delta, the accepted-design scope and trace coverage,\nand the rule-determined treatment. The owning work carrier references that\nexact result and its residuals; a new role does not repeat an unchanged\nwalk (P4b, P2; rc.2 #upward-propagation-check).",
          "section_id":"repo://stdo-v3-generation/claude/9aee01ffa76cfbde1a6b8f1153a25bd6d71c216e/source/standards/TICKETS.md#TICKETS%2F4.%20Ticket%20rules%2FUpward%20propagation%20check",
          "start_line":482
        }
      ],
      "key":"upward_propagation",
      "label":"Upward propagation check"
    },
    {
      "conditions": [],
      "definition": "The minimum ticket status population is backlog, active and completed. Ticket/local statuses, lanes, sprint statuses, migration_strategy, library_usage and recorded change_class are declared carrier states, not evaluation results. Backlog is accepted durable work outside current focus; active is the bounded execution set; completed is closed historical work.",
      "exceptions": [],
      "grounding": [
        {
          "end_line":540,
          "quote":"The ticket statuses, any local state a project adds, the lanes below, the\nsprint statuses of §3 and the `migration_strategy` and `library_usage`\nvalues of §6 are declared states of their carriers, not results, and map\nto no core value; the recorded `change_class` is a declared state under\n[SPEC_METHOD §4](SPEC_METHOD.md#change-classes).",
          "section_id":"repo://stdo-v3-generation/claude/9aee01ffa76cfbde1a6b8f1153a25bd6d71c216e/source/standards/TICKETS.md#TICKETS%2F4.%20Ticket%20rules%2FStatus",
          "start_line":536
        },
        {
          "end_line":561,
          "quote":"`backlog`\nis accepted durable work that should remain visible but is not part of the\ncurrent execution focus; `active` is the current bounded execution set;\n`completed` is closed work retained as history.",
          "section_id":"repo://stdo-v3-generation/claude/9aee01ffa76cfbde1a6b8f1153a25bd6d71c216e/source/standards/TICKETS.md#TICKETS%2F4.%20Ticket%20rules%2FLanes",
          "start_line":556
        }
      ],
      "key":"work_carrier_states",
      "label":"Declared work-carrier states and lanes"
    },
    {
      "conditions": [],
      "definition": "One run-scoped contract for every execution governed by the described method. The smallest lawful source carrier is selected by required lifetime after establishing upstream work authority: an exact admitted ticket, durable ticket, admitted sprint with manifest-local iteration, or intake-drafted run contract. Ticket-shaped means required categorisation, closure criteria, proof, non-closure conditions and fields, without creating ticket identity or lane state.",
      "exceptions": [],
      "grounding": [
        {
          "end_line":634,
          "quote":"Every execution governed by this method uses one run-scoped execution\ncontract. The required lifetime of the work or obligation selects the\nsmallest lawful source carrier: first establish the applicable upstream\nwork authority, then select the first matching row below. For work inside\nan admitted sprint the local carrier boundary is that sprint; otherwise it\nis the current run (P2, P4b, P6; rc.2 #work-carrier-to-execution-rule).",
          "section_id":"repo://stdo-v3-generation/claude/9aee01ffa76cfbde1a6b8f1153a25bd6d71c216e/source/standards/TICKETS.md#TICKETS%2F5.%20From%20carrier%20to%20execution%2FCarrier%20selection",
          "start_line":629
        }
      ],
      "key":"execution_contract",
      "label":"Run-scoped execution contract"
    },
    {
      "conditions": [],
      "definition": "F13 explains work-carrier rules through stated commitments and instance premises: lifetimes, several apparent work-state surfaces, several tenants, migrations and variable carrier forms. P0 is recovered; P1, P2, P3, P4a and P6 are independent where cited; P4b derives from P0/P2 with non-reacquirable step facts; P5 derives from P0/P6 with the open-world premise. The table distinguishes consequences, member policy, external ownership and consumers; §10 records dispositions and decides nothing.",
      "exceptions": [],
      "grounding": [
        {
          "end_line":586,
          "quote":"Its instance premises, which the commitments do\nnot supply, are named in the rows as follows: lifetimes, work and its\nobligations last one run, one bounded batch or longer; several surfaces,\ngoals, tickets, comments, boards and summaries can each appear to state\nthe same work state; several tenants, one work item may be realised in\nmore than one build tenant; migrations, some work replaces one\nauthoritative truth path with another across producers, consumers and\nproofs; carriers vary, a project may keep its carriers in files, trackers\nor services.",
          "section_id":"repo://stdo-v3-generation/claude/9aee01ffa76cfbde1a6b8f1153a25bd6d71c216e/source/standards/TICKETS.md#TICKETS%2F5.%20From%20carrier%20to%20execution%2FFoundation%20to%20law%3A%20work%20carriers",
          "start_line":577
        }
      ],
      "key":"work_carrier_foundation_family",
      "label":"F13 work-carrier foundation-to-law account"
    },
    {
      "conditions": [],
      "definition": "The table's eleven consumer routes respectively concern: lifetime selection and carrier binding (SPEC_METHOD, PRODUCT_DEFINITION, CONFORMANCE 41, judgment continuity); admitted execution (ACTIVATION, ODD); authority/projections (PRODUCT_DEFINITION, recommended shapes); sprint batching (SPEC_METHOD, UX, CONFORMANCE 48, status, closure and manifest); triage (SPEC_METHOD, sprint admission, carrier selection and ticket shape); tenant lifecycles (SPEC_METHOD §11, PRODUCT_DEFINITION); migration (DESIGN_MODULE, status and migration shape); assessment/rulings (EVALUATION, EXECUTIVE_BASELINE, computed classification, recurrence); closure/tests (SPEC_METHOD consistency/evidence, EXECUTIVE_BASELINE, ASSURANCE, milestones); term binding (internal admission/closure only); carrier shapes/reading (no separate consumer). Consumers apply owning rules without adding decisions.",
      "exceptions": [],
      "grounding": [
        {
          "end_line":610,
          "quote":"Consumers of these rows, where an actor enters them (FOUNDATIONS §3\nFoundation to law, Consumers), one list per row, each consumer linked to\nits own locus:",
          "section_id":"repo://stdo-v3-generation/claude/9aee01ffa76cfbde1a6b8f1153a25bd6d71c216e/source/standards/TICKETS.md#TICKETS%2F5.%20From%20carrier%20to%20execution%2FFoundation%20to%20law%3A%20work%20carriers",
          "start_line":608
        },
        {
          "end_line":625,
          "quote":"Each consumer applies the rule and decides nothing the owning section does\nnot (P2, P6).",
          "section_id":"repo://stdo-v3-generation/claude/9aee01ffa76cfbde1a6b8f1153a25bd6d71c216e/source/standards/TICKETS.md#TICKETS%2F5.%20From%20carrier%20to%20execution%2FFoundation%20to%20law%3A%20work%20carriers",
          "start_line":624
        }
      ],
      "key":"work_carrier_consumer_routes",
      "label":"Work-carrier consumer routes"
    },
    {
      "conditions": [],
      "definition": "Admission validates a contract against its cited product outcome, prerequisite or experiment basis without admitting that basis. Contract states include drafted, admitted, rejected and superseded. Basis admission or renewal belongs to the selected goals-wave owner or explicitly bounded proxy and remains durably legible in existing goals or ticket authority.",
      "exceptions": [],
      "grounding": [
        {
          "end_line":705,
          "quote":"Admission validates a contract against its cited product outcome,\nprerequisite or experiment basis and does not admit that basis (ACTIVATION\n§1). Admission or renewal of the basis belongs to the authority owning the\nselected goals work wave, or its explicitly bounded proxy, and stays\ndurably legible in the existing goals or ticket authority",
          "section_id":"repo://stdo-v3-generation/claude/9aee01ffa76cfbde1a6b8f1153a25bd6d71c216e/source/standards/TICKETS.md#TICKETS%2F5.%20From%20carrier%20to%20execution%2FExecution%20contract%20admission",
          "start_line":698
        }
      ],
      "key":"contract_admission",
      "label":"Contract admission distinct from basis admission"
    },
    {
      "conditions": [],
      "definition": "Evaluation criteria identify progress/completion, while non-closure conditions identify continuing failure despite local green proof; together they are the work's local gain function.",
      "exceptions": [],
      "grounding": [
        {
          "end_line":714,
          "quote":"An execution contract should expose both what counts as progress or\ncompletion and what still counts as failure even when some local proof is\ngreen. At minimum it should make `evaluation_criteria` and\n`non_closure_conditions` visible. These are the local gain function for\nthe work",
          "section_id":"repo://stdo-v3-generation/claude/9aee01ffa76cfbde1a6b8f1153a25bd6d71c216e/source/standards/TICKETS.md#TICKETS%2F5.%20From%20carrier%20to%20execution%2FEvaluation%20criteria%20and%20non-closure%20conditions",
          "start_line":709
        }
      ],
      "key":"work_evaluation_conditions",
      "label":"Evaluation and non-closure conditions"
    },
    {
      "conditions": [],
      "definition": "Ticket and work-instruction terms are resolved under PRODUCT_DEFINITION §5 using the cited product, re-entry surface, execution basis, affected scope and any authorised semantic relation.",
      "exceptions": [],
      "grounding": [
        {
          "end_line":736,
          "quote":"Ticket and work-instruction terms resolve under the bounded-context\nsemantic resolution of PRODUCT_DEFINITION §5. A ticket may use an\nunqualified term when its cited product, re-entry surface, execution basis\nand affected scope select exactly one bounded-context meaning",
          "section_id":"repo://stdo-v3-generation/claude/9aee01ffa76cfbde1a6b8f1153a25bd6d71c216e/source/standards/TICKETS.md#TICKETS%2F5.%20From%20carrier%20to%20execution%2FBounded-context%20term%20binding",
          "start_line":732
        }
      ],
      "key":"ticket_term_binding",
      "label":"Bounded-context term binding"
    },
    {
      "conditions": ["ticket_category is implementation_migration"],
      "definition": "A ticket-local authority surface containing an explicit migration declaration and live Markdown checklist. The declaration names strategy, exact affected scope, every excluded/disjoint product or compatibility scope and deterministic routing, old/new truth paths or contracts, both old/new producer and consumer sets, every derived projection/report/status/proof surface, and migration closure law.",
      "exceptions": [],
      "grounding": [
        {
          "end_line":771,
          "quote":"The declaration states: the migration strategy used for this work item; the\nexact affected migration scope; every explicitly excluded or disjoint\nproduct or compatibility scope with its deterministic routing relation; the\nold truth path or superseded contract; the new truth path or authoritative\ncontract; the producer set for the old and the new path; the consumer set\nfor the old and the new path; every projection, report, status surface and\nproof surface derived from that contract family; and the closure law for\nthe migration.",
          "section_id":"repo://stdo-v3-generation/claude/9aee01ffa76cfbde1a6b8f1153a25bd6d71c216e/source/standards/TICKETS.md#TICKETS%2F6.%20Implementation%20migration%2FDeclaration%20and%20checklist",
          "start_line":764
        }
      ],
      "key":"migration_ticket",
      "label":"Implementation migration ticket"
    },
    {
      "conditions": [],
      "definition": "Upstream migration tickets publish or admit the new authoritative source carrier. Downstream tickets harden prompt assembly, projections, reports, dossiers, review surfaces and other consumers. Their authored dependency chain runs source carrier to downstream consumers.",
      "exceptions": [],
      "grounding": [
        {
          "end_line":455,
          "quote":"When one migration\nwave introduces a new source carrier and then its prompt, projection or\nread-model consumers, the dependency chain runs `source carrier ->\ndownstream consumers`, never the reverse.",
          "section_id":"repo://stdo-v3-generation/claude/9aee01ffa76cfbde1a6b8f1153a25bd6d71c216e/source/standards/TICKETS.md#TICKETS%2F4.%20Ticket%20rules%2FInside-out%20sequencing",
          "start_line":452
        }
      ],
      "key":"migration_source_ticket",
      "label":"Migration source-carrier ticket"
    },
    {
      "conditions": [],
      "definition": "Migration work on consumers downstream of the source-carrier ticket, including prompt, projection, read-model and review surfaces.",
      "exceptions": [],
      "grounding": [
        {
          "end_line":449,
          "quote":"Tickets that harden prompt assembly, projections, reports, dossiers, review\nsurfaces or other downstream consumers depend on those upstream source\ntickets.",
          "section_id":"repo://stdo-v3-generation/claude/9aee01ffa76cfbde1a6b8f1153a25bd6d71c216e/source/standards/TICKETS.md#TICKETS%2F4.%20Ticket%20rules%2FInside-out%20sequencing",
          "start_line":447
        }
      ],
      "key":"migration_consumer_ticket",
      "label":"Downstream migration ticket"
    },
    {
      "conditions": [],
      "definition": "Migration strategy values are inside_out_hard_break and fundamental_re_adoption. Library-use values are none, consume and extend, distinguishing local rebuilding, consumption and extension of a reusable library/commonisation surface before local implementation lands.",
      "exceptions": [],
      "grounding": [
        {
          "end_line":800,
          "quote":"The required fields are `migration_strategy`, with the values\n`inside_out_hard_break` and `fundamental_re_adoption`, and\n`library_usage`, with the values `none`, `consume` and `extend`.",
          "section_id":"repo://stdo-v3-generation/claude/9aee01ffa76cfbde1a6b8f1153a25bd6d71c216e/source/standards/TICKETS.md#TICKETS%2F6.%20Implementation%20migration%2FDeclaration%20and%20checklist",
          "start_line":798
        },
        {
          "end_line":864,
          "quote":"An implementation migration ticket declares whether the active boundary is\nrebuilding a pattern locally, consuming an existing reusable library or\ncommonisation surface, or extending that surface before the local\nimplementation lands; `library_usage` records which",
          "section_id":"repo://stdo-v3-generation/claude/9aee01ffa76cfbde1a6b8f1153a25bd6d71c216e/source/standards/TICKETS.md#TICKETS%2F6.%20Implementation%20migration%2FRecurring%20realisation%20and%20library%20declaration",
          "start_line":860
        }
      ],
      "key":"migration_strategy_library_domains",
      "label":"Migration strategy and library-use domains"
    },
    {
      "conditions": [],
      "definition": "A ticket-specific review surface for functional migration questions, a named interface-by-interface closure checklist, ordered breaks and break-to-closure mappings. These are described review operations and document roles, not performed reviews.",
      "exceptions": [],
      "grounding": [
        {
          "end_line":948,
          "quote":"For a core-interface, carrier or authoritative-contract migration, the\nticket includes an `Impacted Interface Review Checklist` before tests can be\nused as closure evidence. The checklist is a named walk of every live\ninterface that can still bypass, reinterpret, replay or project the\nmigrating truth path",
          "section_id":"repo://stdo-v3-generation/claude/9aee01ffa76cfbde1a6b8f1153a25bd6d71c216e/source/standards/TICKETS.md#TICKETS%2F6.%20Implementation%20migration%2FImpacted%20interface%20review%20checklist",
          "start_line":944
        }
      ],
      "key":"migration_review_surfaces",
      "label":"Migration review and execution documents"
    },
    {
      "conditions": [],
      "definition": "Independent assessment is distinct from authoring, self-review and the decision owner's acceptance. Tier-1 assessment/acceptance laws are cited to EVALUATION §§3,5 and closed-result reuse to ACTIVATION §7; this member specifies their work-carrier consequences.",
      "exceptions": [],
      "grounding": [
        {
          "end_line":1044,
          "quote":"The generic law is tier 1: a constructor's self-review is construction\noutput and the independent-assessment protocol is\n[EVALUATION §3](EVALUATION.md#3-independent-assessment); acceptance as the\ndecision owner's disposition, and closure by stated condition rather than\nby reviewer approval, is [EVALUATION §5](EVALUATION.md#5-acceptance); reuse\nof a closed result is [ACTIVATION §7](ACTIVATION.md#7-closed-result-and-reuse).\nThis section states what those laws require of work carriers.",
          "section_id":"repo://stdo-v3-generation/claude/9aee01ffa76cfbde1a6b8f1153a25bd6d71c216e/source/standards/TICKETS.md#TICKETS%2F7.%20Authority%2C%20judgment%2C%20closure%20and%20state",
          "start_line":1038
        }
      ],
      "key":"independent_work_assessment",
      "label":"Independent assessment and acceptance distinction"
    },
    {
      "conditions": [],
      "definition": "Reusable records in the owning work carrier: residual judgment J carries its question, exact subject/basis, computed facts/evidence, actor/authority, conclusion/scope, uncertainty and concrete revising observation; owner ruling O carries original wording, owner, subject/basis, permitted scope/effects, conditions and source separately from agent interpretation. C, J and O are source annotations, not newly instantiated judgment populations.",
      "exceptions": [],
      "grounding": [
        {
          "end_line":1117,
          "quote":"A residual judgment that another activation needs is recorded once, with\nits owning work, in the existing carrier selected under §5. The record\nidentifies the question; the exact subject and governing basis; the\nsupporting computed facts and evidence; the responsible actor and its\nauthority; the conclusion and its scope; the uncertainty; and a concrete\nrevising observation that lets a consumer determine whether the conclusion\nstill applies.",
          "section_id":"repo://stdo-v3-generation/claude/9aee01ffa76cfbde1a6b8f1153a25bd6d71c216e/source/standards/TICKETS.md#TICKETS%2F7.%20Authority%2C%20judgment%2C%20closure%20and%20state%2FRecorded%20judgment%20and%20owner-ruling%20continuity",
          "start_line":1109
        }
      ],
      "key":"recorded_judgment_owner_ruling",
      "label":"Recorded judgment and owner ruling"
    },
    {
      "conditions": [],
